NAME
     wm - a window manager

SYNOPSIS
     wm			- normal start
     wm hardcopy_file	- use 'hardcopy_file' as a hardcopy file
     wm -h		- print this help message

DESCRIPTION
     The window manager 'wm' interprets escape sequences generated by
     ordinary application programs to manipulate multiple windows. Those
     escape sequences do not depend on terminals you are using.  They
     are the mixture of vi200 escape sequences and vt100 ones in addition
     with window control sequences.

     A new window can be created by the following escape sequence (in the
     following, ESC stands for the escape character, ascii code 27):

	    i)	ESC { #w ; #x1 ; #x2 ; #y1 ; #y2 C
	   ii)	ESC { #w ; #x1 ; #x2 ; #y1 ; #y2 ; #p C

     where (#x1,#y1) is the coordinate of the top left corner of the window
     and (#x2,#y2) is that of the bottom right corner.  Note that the top
     left corner of the screen is (0,0).  #w is the window number (currently
     from 0 to 99) which identifies each window.  The window 0 always refers
     to the original base window which is created when you invoke the window
     manager and has the same size of your terminal's screen.  If you use the
     second form ii), you can specify the number of extra pages to the window.
     In the case of i), no extra pages are available.  If a window has extra
     pages, when you fill one window, it goes to the next page, and you can
     come back to the previous page and see it.  For multi-page windows, it
     does not scroll when you output a linefeed at the bottom line.  Instead,
     it goes to the next page and the cursor is at the top line.  This can be
     changed by 'ESC ( #f A' command.

     After creating a new window, all the output are to be displayed in the
     new window.  If you want to select another window, you can do it by
     the following command.

		ESC { #w S	- select the window #w

     You can delete unnecessary windows by:

		ESC { #w D	- delete the window #w

     If you want to reposition the windows,

		ESC { #w P	- put the window #w on top of all the
				  windows
		ESC { #w ; P	- move the window #w to bottom
		ESC { #w1 ; #w2 P
				- put the window #w1 on top of #w2
		ESC { #w ; #x ; #y R
				- move the window #w so that the top left
				  corner becomes (#x,#y)

     Paging commands are:

		ESC { #w ; #p p	- select the page #p of the window #w
		ESC { #w ; #p f	- forward #p pages
		ESC { #w ; #p b	- backward #p pages

     There is a command which enquires about windows.

		ESC { #w ; #f E	- enquire about the window #w

     If #f is not given or is equal to zero, the window manager returns the
     following sequence.

		ESC { #w ; #x ; #y ; #bx ; #by ; #c ; #l e

     Otherwise it returns

		ESC { #w ; #x ; #y ; #bx ; #by ; #c ; #l ; #pl ; #nl ; #ml e

     where #w is the window number, (#x,#y) is the cursor position,
     (#bx,#by) is the position of the top left corner, #c is the column
     length, #l is the line length, #pl is the number of lines preceding
     the current page, #nl is the number of lines following the current
     page and #ml is the maximum auxiliary lines.

     In order to handle several window commands easily, there is 'Mouse
     Mode'.  If you press the escape key twice, you go into the mouse mode.
     The following mouse menu is displayed at the top right corner and
     the cursor indicates the mouse position.

			,Mouse Mode,
			|w = 5     |
			|p = 3     |
			|F1 Create |
			|F2 Delete |
			|F3 Move   |
			|F4 Back   |
			|F5 Next   |
			|F6 Push   |
			|F7 PSelect|
			|F8 PNext  |
			|F9 Copy   |
			`----------'

     The number following 'w =' is the window number which the mouse is
     pointing now, and 'p =' shows its page number.  The mouse can be moved
     by the cursor arrow keys.  Quick move can be achieved after pressing
     the home key (4 chars, 16 chars, 64 chars, according to how many times
     you press the home key).  In order to create a window, move the mouse
     to the position which you want to be one corner of the window, press
     the function key 1, input the window number, press return, input
     auxiliary page number, move the mouse to the position which is the
     the other corner of the window, and press the function key 1 again.
     If you do not give the window number, the window manager assigns the
     lowest number available.  The function key 2 deletes the window which
     is being pointed by the mouse.  The function key 3 moves a window; first
     point the window by the mouse; press the function key 3; move the mouse
     to the new position; press the function key 3 again.  It also puts the
     window on top of the all the others.  The function key 4 shows the
     previous page, and the function key 5 shows the next page.  The function
     key 6 places the window at the bottom.  The function key 9 takes a snap
     shot of the current screen and append it to the hardcopy file.  The
     default file name is 'hardcopy' in the directory in which you call the
     window manager.  The file name can be specified when you invoke the
     window manager.

     If you application program does not like users to use some of mouse
     commands, the following command control that.

		ESC (#f M	- set mouse capability

     where #f consists of 9 bits each of which corresponds to:

		bit 0	- enable mouse mode
		bit 1	- create window
		bit 2	- delete window
		bit 3	- move window
		bit 4	- page backward
		bit 5	- page forward
		bit 6	- push window to bottom
		bit 9	- hardcopy

     At the beginning, all the switches are on.

     The window manager also supplies the escape sequences concerning cursor
     movement and clearing windows.  The basic cursor movement escape
     sequences are:

		ESC H		- cursor home, i.e. (0,0)
		ESC [ A		- cursor up
		ESC A		- cursor up
		ESC [ B		- cursor down
		ESC B		- cursor down
		ESC [ C		- cursor right
		ESC C		- cursor right
		ESC [ D		- cursor left
		ESC D		- cursor left

     In each case, if the cursor is at the beginning or at the end and there
     is no room to move, the position is not changed.  If you use 'LF'
     (linefeed, control-J, ascii 10) instead of 'ESC B' or use 'ESC LF'
     instead of 'ESC A', the window may be scrolled to show the next or
     previous page.

		LF		- cursor down or scroll up
		ESC LF		- cursor up or scroll down

     The direct cursor addressing is available either by

		ESC [ #y ; #x f
		ESC [ #y ; #x H

     where #y and #x is the decimal numbers of raw+1 and column+1 (remember
     that (columne,raw)=(0,0) is the top left corner), respectively, or by

		ESC Y $y $x

     where $y and $x need to be characters whose ascii codes are
     raw+32 and column+32, respectively.

     Escape sequences for clearing lines and windows are:

		ESC [ K		- clear to the end of line
		ESC K		- same as ESC [ K
		ESC x		- same as ESC [ K
		ESC [ J		- clear to the end of current page
		ESC J		- same as ESC [ J
		ESC y		- same as ESC [ J
		ESC [ 2 J	- clear the current page
		ESC v		- same as ESC [ 2 J
		ESC [ 3 J	- clear to the end of last page
		ESC [ 4 J	- clear all pages

     Insertion and deletion are:

		ESC L		- insert a line
		ESC [ L		- same as ESC L
		ESC M		- delete a line
		ESC [ M		- same as ESC M
		ESC i		- insert mode on
		ESC [ h		- same as ESC i
		ESC j		- insert mode off
		ESC [ l		- same as ESC j
		ESC O		- delete one character
		ESC [ #p P	- delete #p characters

     If the terminal has some kind of highlight facility, you can use
     the next escape sequence.

		ESC [ 1 m	- start highlighting
		ESC [ m		- finish highlighting

     You can prevent several top lines to scroll so that you can use them
     as a heading of the window.

		ESC r		- set the scroll start line at the current
				  cursor position

     There is another way of giving a heading if it can be fitted in a line.
     The following command places the string on the frame of the window.

		ESC { #w H " $s "

     where $s is a string whose length is less that or equal to the line
     length.   The string must be bracketted (like [...]) or quoted by the
     same character (like "...").

     Each window has several optional switches you can change by the following
     command.

		ESC { #w ; #f A	- change the optional switches of window #w
		ESC ( #f A	- change the optional switches of the
				  current window

     where #f consists of 6 bits each of which corresponds to a particular
     switch.

		bit 0	- auto new line (default on)
		bit 1	- scroll enable (default on for single-page window,
			  off for multi-page window)
		bit 2	- refresh after each newline (default off)
		bit 3	- scroll page by page (default off for single-page
			  window, on for multi-page window)
		bit 4	- highlight (default off)
		bit 5	- insert mode (default off)

     The window manager normally refreshes the screen when it has time to
     do.  Therefore, when your program outputs a lot of data continuously,
     they may not appear on the screen at all and you can see only the last
     several lines.  If you set the bit 2 switch above, the window manager
     refreshes at least after receiving newline (control-J) character.  On
     the other hand if you do not like update the screen until you want to do,
     the window manager can be set to dump mode.

		ESC ( 1 D	- set dump mode
		ESC ( D		- reset dump mode

     In the dump mode, the screen manager do not update any windows until
     it receives the set dump mode command again.

     In addition to the uniform screen interface, the window manager also
     supply uniform interface to function keys.  If you press a function key
     or arrow key, the window manager translate it into:

		function key 0 : ESC 0
		function key 1 : ESC 1
		function key 2 : ESC 2
		function key 3 : ESC 3
		function key 4 : ESC 4
		function key 5 : ESC 5
		function key 6 : ESC 6
		function key 7 : ESC 7
		function key 8 : ESC 8
		function key 9 : ESC 9
		up arrow key   : ESC A
		down arrow key : ESC B
		right arrow key: ESC C
		left arrow key : ESC D
		home key       : ESC H

     The translation of function keys (not arrow keys) above is default.
     You can change it by the following escape sequence.

		ESC ( #f ; #m F " $s "

     where #f is the function key number (from 0 to 9); if #m is non-zero,
     the function key is processed locally; $s is the definition string either
     quoted by a character or bracketted.

     The followings are not strongly recommended to use, but they are easy
     to use.  If you can write a program which does 'stty' system call,
     it should be used instead.

		ESC ( E		- terminal echo off
		ESC ( 1 E	- terminal echo on
		ESC ( R		- terminal cooked mode
		ESC ( 1 R	- terminal raw mode

     As we have seen that the function key 8 in the mouse mode provides a
     hardcopy facility, you can also use the next escape sequence from
     your program to take a snap shot of the screen.

		ESC ( #a C " $f "

     where $f is the file name; if #a is non-zero, the file will be over
     written; if #a is not given or zero, the snap shot will be appended
     to the file.  Each snap shot will be separated by the formfeed character
     (control-L, ascii code 12), and highlighted portions on the screen will
     be underlined.

     Finally, since the window manager changes the terminal configuration into
     the uniform one, you need to change the terminal name in the environment
     in order to use some application programs which look up the TERMCAP entry.
     The following is a recommended TERMCAP entry of the terminal under the
     window manager.

	ideal|ideal terminal for window manager:\
		:co#80:li#24:am:cr=^M:do=^J:nl=^J:bl=^G:\
		:al=\EL:bs:cd=\Ey:ce=\Ex:cl=\Ev:\
		:cm=\E[%i%d;%dH:dc=\EO:dl=\EM:ho=\EH:so=\E[7m:se=\E[m:\
		:k0=\E0:k1=\E1:k2=\E2:k3=\E3:k4=\E4:k5=\E5:k6=\E6:\
		:k7=\E7:k8=\E8:k9=\E9:ku=\EA:kd=\EB:kr=\EC:kl=\ED:kh=\EH:\
		:nd=\EC:ta=^I:pt:up=\EA:vs=\Ed:ve=\Ec:

     'co' and 'li' need to be modified according to the window size.  For
     convenience, if you are using 'csh' as your unix command processor,
     the window manager generate the following sequence after initialization.

		setenv TERM $wterm

     Therefore, you put 'ideal' into your TERMCAP, and the following line
     into your '.cshrc' file.

		set wterm=ideal

     If you do not have your own TERMCAP file, you can cheet by adding

		set wterm=vi200

     or

		set wterm=vt100

     the next line into '.cshrc'.

     Other rubbish commands which have never been used.

		ESC R		- redraw the screen
		ESC ( #c K	- set #c to kill character
				  (default control-\, ascii 28)
		ESC ( K		- reset kill character

FILES
     ./hardcopy		default hardcopy file
     <wm>/wm.hlp	help message file
     <wm>/wm.c		source program (main)
     <wm>/winlib.h
     <wm>/winlib.c	window manipulation library
     <wm>/display.h
     <wm>/display.c	screen management package (from Gosling EMACS)
     <wm>/term.h
     <wm>/term.c	terminal control module (from Gosling EMACS)

LIMITATIONS
     A lot!!

SEE ALSO
     Subroutine packages for some programming languages are available.

